 "title": "Foundations of Reinforcement Learning with Applications in Finance",
 "byline": "Ashwin Rao and Tikhon Jelvis \u00b7 CRC Press / Chapman & Hall, 2022 (authors\u2019 free PDF)",
 "note": "Short appendix chapters (moment generating functions, portfolio theory, stochastic calculus, HJB, Black\u2013Scholes, affine spaces, conjugate priors) and per-chapter key-takeaway sections are not broken out. Summaries are in our own words; the text stays in the PDF.",
 "summary": "A Stanford-course textbook that teaches reinforcement learning from Markov processes up, with Python implementations built on careful abstractions and with finance as the running application area. After an overview and a chapter on code design, Module I covers Markov processes, MRPs and MDPs, dynamic programming, and function approximation with approximate DP. Module II casts financial problems as MDPs: utility theory, Merton-style asset allocation, derivatives pricing and hedging (including American options and incomplete markets), and order-book execution and market-making. Module III presents RL algorithms: MC and TD prediction and control, batch methods (experience replay, DQN, LSPI, gradient TD) and policy gradients. Module IV covers bandits, blending learning with planning (MCTS) and real-world considerations."
}